WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
Good morning.

2
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:04.040
Afternoon, evening, wherever you are.

3
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.560
And my name is Maya Sarfaty.

4
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.960
I'm a PhD candidate at the Faculty of Fine
Arts, University of Lisbon.

5
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.560
The title of my talk
today is Hosting The Gap Digital

6
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.800
Typography
as Linguistic Hospitality in Translation.

7
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.080
This talk is part of a larger

8
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.920
artistic research project
called Mother's Cry in the same language.

9
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.840
The project works with multilingual
maternal testimonies shaped by war

10
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:38.160
and loss, before working with
the verbal testimonies of other women.

11
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:43.320
I am first seeking to stabilize
the methodology for this reason.

12
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:44.440
At this stage,

13
00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.920
all experiments are conducted exclusively
with my own text and testimony.

14
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.960
Today I will focus on a screen based video
essay, moving mainly between Hebrew

15
00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.920
and Portuguese, with a smaller
listening experiment involving Arabic.

16
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.600
I will examine how digital typography
can make translational gaps perceptible

17
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.720
in reading, rather than allowing
fluent translation to conceal them.

18
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.560
Translation always changes something.

19
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.080
It involves decisions, shifts,
losses, transformations, and new meaning.

20
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.120
But when it translates, translation
reads fluently.

21
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.600
These changes may become difficult
to perceive.

22
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.320
The translated sentence appear complete.

23
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.400
It seemed to replace the source.

24
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.480
My research begins
from this reading problem.

25
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.520
I'm not asking how typography can

26
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.440
illustrate the translation
or how it can decorate translated text.

27
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:40.400
I'm asking whether deeper graphic
movement, spacing, rhythm, density,

28
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.080
and delay
can allow the reader to encounter

29
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.560
something that fluent language
would otherwise smooth away.

30
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.280
So the central question is,
how can digital typography make unresolved

31
00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.320
translational gaps perceptible in reading?

32
00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.560
In my daily work as an international

33
00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.520
PhD student, translation
first appeared as a practical necessity.

34
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.360
I used the translation
because it was accessible and immediate,

35
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.320
but I do not treat
LMS as the central object of my research

36
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.000
or as a natural tools.

37
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:11.920
I approach them

38
00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.400
as part of the contemporary condition
of automated fluent language production.

39
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.880
It produces a fluent
and statistically probable surface.

40
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.600
This fluency is useful precisely
because it gives me something to question.

41
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:30.560
I then return to the source text
and ask what has become less perceptible?

42
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.760
What emotional, syntactic,
cultural, phonetic

43
00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.520
or relational differences
has been normalized?

44
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.320
The therefore

45
00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.760
not where translation ends,
it is where critical reading begins.

46
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.760
The identification of significant

47
00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:50.440
gaps remains a human, interactive
and ethical decision.

48
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.560
In this sense, supervision is not control
over translation,

49
00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.560
but attentive human intervention
in what fluency conceals.

50
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.280
The working process
currently has five stages.

51
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.360
Trust and initial translation
creates a fluent vision.

52
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:10.480
Second,
I compare the languages through close

53
00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.800
human reading and identify differences
that may matter.

54
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.480
Not every difference becomes a gap.

55
00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.160
A gap is a difference
that becomes significant

56
00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:22.320
within the specific text
and reading situation.

57
00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.840
Third, I reflect on whether this gap
should receive an intervention at all.

58
00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.080
Fourth, I develop a Tipografia response,
and fifth,

59
00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.520
I return to the result
and ask what it has done.

60
00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.400
Did it make the gap perceptible?

61
00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.320
Did it hold the gap

62
00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:45.840
without explaining or solving it,
or did it become only a visual effect?

63
00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.120
The experiment
is therefore not only an artwork,

64
00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.280
it is also the place
where the method is being built.

65
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.320
I will now show a short segment

66
00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.320
from the video.

67
00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.880
At the.

68
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Research IMI.

69
00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.800
The site in me of.

70
00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.960
Of Pacific.

71
00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.120
The first sequence

72
00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.840
began as listening experiment
across languages.

73
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:31.080
I placed words related to listening

74
00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.920
and silence beside one
another in Portuguese, Hebrew and Arabic.

75
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.920
They may occupy a similar semantic area,
but they do not carry

76
00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:43.120
the same sound, rhythm, command, bodily
pressure, or cultural tone.

77
00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.840
The aim was not to claim equivalence,
it was to listen to how

78
00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.080
apparently related words moved differently
when the experiment moved

79
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.880
from individual words
into a complete sentence.

80
00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.800
A deeper structural gap appeared.

81
00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.960
A possible English translation
I am learning

82
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.280
to write my longing for my mother's tongue
in the other language.

83
00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.000
Several gaps appeared at once.

84
00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.040
One considered

85
00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.840
word order in Hebrew,
other closes the sentence,

86
00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.200
but for occurred in Portuguese Notre
lingua.

87
00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.880
Places other before language.

88
00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:23.680
The sentence ends differently,

89
00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.840
which changes the rhythm
and the final emotional pressure.

90
00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:31.880
The second gap is in My Mother's Son.

91
00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:37.080
The standard Portuguese
phrase lingua moderna fell to categorical.

92
00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.360
The mother becomes an adjective,
while in Hebrew

93
00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:42.640
she remains concrete and intimate.

94
00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.120
So I chose lingua dominion ma,
the language of my mother

95
00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.480
as a form of linguistic.

96
00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:52.600
I'm not claiming
this is the only possible translation.

97
00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.120
I chose it because it keeps the concrete
mother present.

98
00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.960
But this produced a new gap.

99
00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:03.480
Hebrew compresses language mother
and belonging into one intimate unit.

100
00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:06.400
Portuguese unfolds them as a chain.

101
00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.560
This is not only syntactic,
it is a structural difference

102
00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:14.320
in how each language builds relation
between self, mother and language.

103
00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:16.800
I chose to

104
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.800
respond
slipper graphically to these two stroke

105
00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:22.560
gaps and not to the phonetic gap.

106
00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.800
Some gaps are addressed through
translation and translation choices.

107
00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:31.560
Some people graphically
and some without intervention.

108
00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:37.040
A second example concerns
emotional weight.

109
00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:40.480
Let's see.

110
00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.080
The Hebrew phrase is.

111
00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.000
It could be understood
as a soft, gentle, or pleasant longing.

112
00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:32.040
In Portuguese
it became so that color roja,

113
00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:37.480
but so that carries a particular cultural
and emotional history.

114
00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:43.040
It may feel ever more melancholic or more
saturated than the Hebrew word god.

115
00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:46.040
In this sentence,

116
00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:50.080
there is also a gap between name, and

117
00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:53.320
the Hebrew suggests softness
or gentleness.

118
00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:57.560
The Portuguese move moves toward
warmth and density.

119
00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:03.360
I ask whether the intervention
would be a speech or intrusion.

120
00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:06.640
I chose to intervene,
but not to restore balance.

121
00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.800
I wanted the asymmetric to remain visible.

122
00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:14.000
The Tipografia response places
the Hebrew phrase

123
00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:17.840
as something smaller and lighter
within the larger Portuguese form.

124
00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.880
It does not restore balance.

125
00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:23.280
It holds the imbalance
and allows the reader to encounter

126
00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.400
the unequal emotional weight
of the two expressions.

127
00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.240
I understand linguistic hospitality

128
00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.720
not as observing difference
into a fluent target language,

129
00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.840
but as a.

130
00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:41.760
Difference to remain active within reach.

131
00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:43.760
I'm in space for this encounter.

132
00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:45.960
It can make the translated text acetate

133
00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:49.840
expand, compress full, or resist
complete equivalence.

134
00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:53.640
The gap is is therefore
not only a failure of translation,

135
00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:56.600
it becomes the material of the work.

136
00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:57.880
Thank you very much.
